-- Rust binding for the mirror's `Forge` carrier (proposal 038 / WI-1117).
--
-- SEPARATE FROM THE DECLARATION, on the `anthill.persistence` precedent: the
-- spec and its entities are language-agnostic and live in `coordination.anthill`;
-- WHICH HOST FUNCTION realizes each operation, and WHICH TARGETS this host
-- realizes at all, live here.
--
-- IT IS NOT TIDINESS. A binding block naming a host function the runtime does
-- not have is FATAL — no interpreter can be built for the program, at any call
-- site. The five functions below are `anthill-todo`'s own, registered on the KB
-- before load through the WI-1122 seam, so a reader that loads the domain WITHOUT
-- this binary — anthill-core's own `store.anthill` type-check fixtures do exactly
-- that — must be able to take the declaration and leave the binding. Merged into
-- one file, adding `MirrorEntry` to `store.anthill`'s imports broke ten
-- anthill-core tests, which is how this was found.

-- ── The rust realization (proposal 038) ─────────────────────────
--
-- THE OPERATIONS ARE COMMON, so they are declared and mapped ONCE — the same
-- shape, and for the same reason, as `anthill.persistence`'s six storage
-- operations in `rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/persistence.anthill`. Each is one
-- rust function taking the target as its first argument and resolving THAT
-- VALUE to the implementation it names. There is no per-target variant to name:
-- `GithubForge` and `FakeForge` reach the same five, so mapping them per carrier
-- would be five signatures copied per target to say the same thing twice.
--
-- WHICH TARGETS THIS HOST REALIZES is the separate question, and the one-line
-- blocks below answer it. A spec-level mapping says the OPERATION has an
-- implementation, never that a given carrier is realized (WI-876) — without the
-- carrier blocks an arbitrary `entity ZzNotAForge(v: Int64)` could claim
-- `fact Forge[ZzNotAForge]` and load clean.
--
-- THE HOST FUNCTIONS ARE THE EMBEDDER'S, NOT THE RUNTIME'S. `HOST_FNS` in
-- anthill-core is a closed `const` slice and knows nothing about forges; putting
-- the `gh` shell-out there would make the kernel learn about them. They are
-- registered on the `KnowledgeBase` by `anthill-todo`'s own `main` before load,
-- through the seam WI-1122 opened — and they are CLOSURES, because the fake
-- needs the project directory to resolve its `dir` against.
namespace anthill.stage0
  provides Forge language rust
    artifact "rustland/anthill-todo/src/forge/mod.rs"
    operation_map {
      target_name:    "forge_target_name",
      create_entry:   "forge_create_entry",
      update_entry:   "forge_update_entry",
      list_entries:   "forge_list_entries",
      entry_comments: "forge_entry_comments"
    }
  end

  provides GithubForge language rust
    artifact "rustland/anthill-todo/src/forge/gh.rs"
  end

  provides FakeForge language rust
    artifact "rustland/anthill-todo/src/forge/fake.rs"
  end

  fact Forge[GithubForge]
  fact Forge[FakeForge]
end
